`parsePrice`:
Need tolerant to symbols/separators, clear error. Website prices "1590.00"; could be "$1,590.00" or "1.590,00". Need parse robustly. Approach:
```ts
export function parsePrice(value: string): number {
  const normalized = value.replace(/\s/g, '').replace(/[^\d.,-]/g, '');
  if (!normalized) throw new Error(`Cannot parse price from "${value}".`);
  const lastComma = normalized.lastIndexOf(',');
  const lastDot = normalized.lastIndexOf('.');
  let canonical: string;
  if (lastComma >= 0 && lastDot >= 0) {
    // last separator is decimal
    if (lastComma > lastDot) {
      canonical = normalized.replace(/\./g, '').replace(',', '.');
    } else {
      canonical = normalized.replace(/,/g, '');
    }
  } else if (lastComma >= 0) {
    // comma only: if 2 decimals -> decimal; else thousands
    const decimals = normalized.length - lastComma - 1;
    canonical = decimals === 2 ? normalized.replace(',', '.') : normalized.replace(/,/g, '');
  } else {
    canonical = normalized;
  }
  const parsed = Number(canonical);
  if (!Number.isFinite(parsed)) throw new Error(...);
  return parsed;
}
```
Need handle negative? Prices not negative. Could reject negative? Not necessary. Handle currency symbols. `replace(/[^\d.,-]/g, '')` retains minus. If value "abc" -> normalized "" -> error. If "abc123" -> "123" -> returns 123, but maybe should error? "tolerante a símbolos y separadores" means strip currency symbols, not letters. Letters should be invalid. We could strip known currency symbols and whitespace, not all non-digits. But currency could be "$", "ARS", "USD". Requirement "tolerante a símbolos y separadores". A robust parser could strip `[^\d.,-]`, which would turn "abc" into "" and error, but "abc123" into 123. That's maybe too tolerant. Better:
